WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.630
- [Narrator] There is plenty of advise

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:04.210
for how to improve your sleep.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.110
Go to bed at the same time,

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:10.053
avoid digital screens after
dark, and don't hit snooze.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.660
But it all comes down
to the same assumption,

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.500
that you're doing something wrong.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.833
When in fact, it may not
be your fault at all.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.270
Inside practically
every organism on earth,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.970
there's a clock that keeps order.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.810
Known as a circadian rhythm.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.620
For humans, it's located
in the part of our brain

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.990
called the hypothalamus.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:42.500
And while it's most famous for
controlling our sleep cycles,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.680
it's also responsible for
helping primary organs

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.970
like the brain, heart,
and lungs work in harmony.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.223
But not everyone's circadian
rhythm is the same.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.410
Night owls for instance,
generally feel tired

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.023
later than early birds.

00:00:59.881 --> 00:01:02.530
Often because they produce high amounts

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.210
of the sleep hormone
melatonin later at night.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.717
And for most of human
history, that didn't matter.

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:12.320
Since night owls could
protect their tribes

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.670
from nocturnal predators,

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.625
or their cities from cunning conquerors.

00:01:16.625 --> 00:01:20.993
But society has made it a
problem in recent decades.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.760
An estimated 80% of Americans
follow daily schedules

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.540
that fall between 6AM and 6PM.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.090
Yet nearly a 1/3 of the population

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.560
considers themselves night owls,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.080
which means they're
better off with a schedule

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.003
that looks more like this.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.633
This phenomenon is called social jetlag.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.314
It's like the jetlag you
feel after a long plane trip

00:01:47.314 --> 00:01:51.603
but worse because it doesn't
disappear after a few days.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.970
And social jetlag is taking its tole

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.230
on the night owls of the world

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.470
because even if you get the
recommended amount of sleep,

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.313
knocking your circadian rhythm
out of wack has consequences.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.950
For example, one study
found that for every hour

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.350
your circadian rhythm is out of sync,

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.590
your risk of obesity increases by 33%.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.740
Also increasing your risk of
the many health complications

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.860
associated with obesity

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.740
and the problem isn't just a physical one.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.435
In another study, people
whose circadian rhythms

00:02:26.435 --> 00:02:29.270
were more than two hours off,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:33.140
reported notably more severe
symptoms of depression.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.760
And since your circadian
rhythm tends to shift

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.760
as you age, social jetlag is
especially apparent in teens.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.690
In fact, the CDC warns
that most public schools

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.120
across America start
too early, before 8:30,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.240
which according to the
non-profit RAND Corporation,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.950
is costing the country
nine billion dollars a year

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.400
from mainly lost academic
performance and car crashes

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.990
from tired teens behind the wheel.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.220
Luckily, the circadian
rhythm isn't set in stone.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.360
Turns out, it's largely
triggered by light signals

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.130
that strike your eye.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.517
So when you first wake up,

00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:16.010
get outside and soak up some morning sun.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.860
Or if that's out of the question,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.350
make sure your home is well-lit.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.107
It might just brighten
your morning a little more.

00:03:23.107 --> 00:03:27.063
We'd like to learn what you
wanna know about the human body.

00:03:27.063 --> 00:03:30.043
Tell us in the comments
and thanks of watching.

